# {{.project_name}}

Agentic RAG application generated from the AAI platform template.

## Local setup

Authenticate without storing credentials:

```bash
az login
export DATABRICKS_HOST={{.workspace_host}}
export DATABRICKS_AUTH_TYPE=azure-cli
python scripts/install_core.py
python -m pip install -r requirements.lock
python -m pip install -e '.[dev]'
pytest -q
```

Copy `aai-platform.yml` for each environment and replace every
`replace-with-*` value before running a provider integration.

## Development lifecycle

1. Use `notebooks/01_explore.py` for exploration, not production orchestration.
2. Put tested application code under `src/app`.
3. Register prompts with `python scripts/register_prompt.py` (sets the
   `development` alias).
4. Replace and grow the evaluation cases under `evals/data/` with your
   domain's golden and failure cases — the seeded cases show the required
   shape (grounded answers, refusals, injection attempts). Keep at least 10;
   grow toward 150+.
5. Run the release gate: `python evals/evaluate.py`. It enforces the safety,
   groundedness, and relevance thresholds in `evals/gate_config.json`, plus
   regression against `evals/baseline.json` once that exists. Pull-request CI
   runs only `evals/offline_checks.py` (deterministic, credential-free); this
   full LLM-judge run needs model access.
6. Promote the evaluated prompt version:
   `python scripts/promote_prompt.py --version <n> --to candidate`, and
   `--to production` after release approval. Production deploys load the
   `production` alias — promotion must happen before the first prod deploy.
7. Record the evaluated code, prompt, model, and retrieval configuration with
   `python scripts/create_release.py`.
8. Validate and deploy with `databricks bundle validate -t dev` and
   `databricks bundle deploy -t dev`. After a released run passes, refresh
   the regression baseline: `python evals/evaluate.py --update-baseline`.

The application uses logical resources (`general-chat`,
`knowledge-embedding`, and `product-knowledge`). Provider endpoints are
configuration, not application code.

The included deploy workflow remains keyless. Before enabling it, the platform
team must onboard the generated repository with its own main-branch FIC,
Databricks service principal, constrained compute permission, and non-secret
repository variables.
